1. Explain the difference between var, let and const keywords.
answer:

var

Old way of declaring variables (before ES6).
It is function-scoped, not block-scoped.
Can be re-declared and updated.
Hoisted (moved to the top during execution) but initialized with undefined.
example:var name = "Vinith";
var name = "Shetty"; // re-declaration allowed
console.log(name);   // Output: Shetty

let

Introduced in ES6.
Block-scoped (works only inside {}).
Can be updated but cannot be re-declared in the same scope.
Hoisted, but not initialized → gives ReferenceError if used before declaration.
Example:
let age = 22;
age = 23; // updating is allowed
// let age = 24; error (cannot re-declare in same scope)
console.log(age); // Output: 23

const

Also ES6.
Block-scoped.
Cannot be updated or re-declared.
Must be initialized at the time of declaration.
Example:
const country = "India";
// country = "Germany";  error (cannot update)
console.log(country); // Output: India

2.Mention the different datatypes with an example for each.
answer:
JavaScript has two main categories:
Primitive Data Types (basic, single value)
Non-Primitive (Reference) Data Types (collections, objects)

Primitive Data Types

String – sequence of characters
let name = "Vinith"; // string


Number – integers, floats, etc.
let age = 22; // number


Boolean – true or false
let isStudent = true;


Undefined – declared but not assigned
let city;
console.log(city); // undefined


Null – explicitly no value
let data = null;


Symbol – unique identifier
let id = Symbol("123");


BigInt – very large numbers
let bigNum = 123456789012345678901234567890n;


Non-Primitive Data Types

Object – collection of key-value pairs
let person = { name: "Vinith", age: 22 };

Array – ordered list of values
let fruits = ["apple", "banana", "mango"];


Function – block of code
function greet() {
  return "Hello!";
}